Recursion
Recursion means a function calling itself.
Every recursive function needs a Base Case to stop.
The Recursive Case is where the function calls itself.
The recursive call should move toward the Base Case.
Without a Base Case, recursion can run forever.
Example: countdown(n) can call countdown(n-1).
Recursion breaks a big problem into smaller versions of the same problem.
Python uses the call stack to keep track of recursive function calls.
When the Base Case is reached, functions start returning back upward.
Remember: Base Case + Smaller Problem + Recursive Call = Recursion.

